====== Leetcode 78. Subsets =======

Step-by-step algorithm to generate all subsets of an array:

Step 1: Initialize an empty list al to store all subsets.

Step 2: Call a recursive function find starting from index 0 with an empty temporary list arl.

Step 3: In the recursive function find:

Sub-step 3a: Add a copy of the current list arl to al.
(This stores the current subset.)

Sub-step 3b: Loop over the elements from st (current start index) to n-1:

Sub-sub-step 3b-i: Add the current element nums[i] to arl.

Sub-sub-step 3b-ii: Recursively call find with the next index i + 1.
(This explores all subsets including the current element.)

Sub-sub-step 3b-iii: Remove the last element from arl.
(This backtracks to explore subsets without the current element.)

Step 4: Continue the recursion until all elements are considered.

Step 5: Return al containing all possible subsets.

Time Complexity:
There are 2^n possible subsets for n elements.
Each subset can take up to O(n) time to copy.
Time Complexity = O(n × 2^n)

Space Complexity:
Recursive call stack can go up to n levels.
Space Complexity = O(n) (excluding output list)


          ---------------> Code <-------------------

class Solution {
    List<List<Integer>> al=new ArrayList<>();
    public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {

        find(nums,0,nums.length,new ArrayList<>());
        return al;
    }
    public void find(int[] nums,int st,int n,List<Integer> arl){
        al.add(new ArrayList<>(arl));
        for(int i=st;i<n;i++){
            arl.add(nums[i]);
            find(nums,i+1,n,arl);
            arl.remove(arl.size()-1);
        }
    }
}
